1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,600
Reflection often improves the performance of the system, but if I commit to keeping it,

2
00:00:04,980 --> 00:00:08,660
I would usually want to double check how much it actually improves the performance because

3
00:00:08,660 --> 00:00:13,200
it does slow down the system a little bit by needing to take an extra step.

4
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:18,500
Let's take a look at evals for reflection workflows. Let's look at an example of using

5
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:25,920
reflection to improve the database query that an LM writes to fetch data to answer questions.

6
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:29,560
Let's say you run a retail store and you may get questions like,

7
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:33,580
which color product has the highest total sales? To answer a question like this,

8
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:40,280
you might have an LM generate a database query. If you've heard of database languages like SQL SQL,

9
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:44,360
it may generate a query in that type of language, but if you're not familiar with SQL,

10
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:50,740
don't worry about it. But after writing a database query, instead of using that directly to fetch

11
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:55,860
information from the database, you may have an LM, the same or different now, reflect on

12
00:00:55,860 --> 00:01:01,260
the version one database query and update it to maybe an improved one and then execute that database

13
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:07,920
query against the database to fetch information to finally have an LM answer the question. So the

14
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:14,940
question is, does using a second LM to reflect and improve on the database or SQL query actually

15
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:22,280
improve the final output? In order to evaluate this, I might collect a set of questions or set of

16
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:28,000
prompts together with ground truth answers. So maybe one would be how many items are sold in May 2025?

17
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:34,340
What's the most expensive item on the inventory? How many styles are carried in my store? And I write

18
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:44,100
down for maybe 10, 15 prompts, the ground truth answer. Then you can run this workflow without reflection.

19
00:01:44,460 --> 00:01:49,640
So without reflection would mean to take the SQL query generated by the first LM and to just see what

20
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:55,340
answer it gives. And with reflection would mean to take the database query generated after the second LM has

21
00:01:55,340 --> 00:02:01,760
reflected on it to see what answer that fetches from the database. And then we can measure the

22
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:08,040
percentage of correct answers from no reflection and with reflection. In this example, no reflection

23
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:13,880
gets the answers right 87% of the time, with reflection gets it right 95% of the time. And this would suggest

24
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:21,180
that reflection is meaningfully improving the quality of the database queries I'm able to get to pull

25
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:26,620
out the correct answer. One thing that developers often end up doing as well is rewrite the reflection

26
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:33,400
prompt. So for example, do you want to add to reflection prompt an instruction to make the database query

27
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:40,780
run faster or make it clearer? Or you may just have different ideas for how to rewrite either the initial

28
00:02:40,780 --> 00:02:47,180
generation prompt or the reflection prompt. Once you put in place evals like this, you can quickly try out

29
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:53,400
different ideas for these prompts and measure the percentage correct your system has as you change

30
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:59,080
the prompts in order to get a sense of which prompts work best for your application. So if you're trying

31
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:05,760
out a lot of prompts, building evals is important. It really helps you have a systematic way to choose

32
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:12,280
between the different prompts you might be considering. But this example is one of when you can use

33
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:19,480
objective evals because there is a right answer. The number of items still was 1,101 and the answer is

34
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:24,860
either right or wrong. How about applications where you need more subjective rather than objective

35
00:03:24,860 --> 00:03:31,320
evaluations? In the plotting example that we saw in the last video, without reflection, we had the stack

36
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:38,020
bar graph. With reflection, we had this graph. But how do we know which plot is actually better? I know

37
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:42,760
I like the latter one better, but with different graphs varying on different dimensions, how do we

38
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:49,800
figure out which one is better? And measuring which of these plots is better is more of a subjective

39
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:57,940
criteria rather than a purely black and white objective criteria. So for these most subjective criteria,

40
00:03:57,940 --> 00:04:04,540
one thing you might do is use an LLM as a judge. And maybe a basic approach to do this might be to feed

41
00:04:04,540 --> 00:04:10,200
both plots into an LLM, a multimodal LLM that can accept two images as input, and just ask it which

42
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:15,460
image is better. It turns out this doesn't work that well. I'll share even better idea in a second.

43
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:21,300
But one thing you could do might be to also give it some criteria by which to evaluate the two plots,

44
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:25,900
such as clarity, how nice looking they are, and so on. But it turns out that there's some known

45
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:31,580
issues with using LLMs to compare two inputs to tell you which one is better. First, it turns out the

46
00:04:31,580 --> 00:04:36,380
answers are often not very good. They could be sensitive to the exact wording of the prompt of

47
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:41,740
the LLM as a judge, and sometimes the rank ordering doesn't correspond that well to human expert

48
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:47,680
judgments. And one manifestation of this is many LLMs will have a position bias. Many LLMs,

49
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:53,220
turns out, will often pick the first option more often than the second option. And in fact,

50
00:04:53,220 --> 00:04:58,640
I've worked a lot of LLMs where given two choices, whichever choice I present first,

51
00:04:59,160 --> 00:05:03,720
it will say the first choice is better. And maybe some LLMs prefer the second option,

52
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:09,200
but I think most LLMs prefer the first option. Instead of asking an LLM to compare a pair of inputs,

53
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:17,100
grading with a rubric can give more consistent results. So for example, you might prompt an LLM to tell it,

54
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:21,520
given a single image, assess the attached image against the quality rubric,

55
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:26,680
and the rubric or grading criteria may have clear criteria like, does the plot have a clear title,

56
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:31,240
are the axis labels present, is the appropriate chart type, and so on, with handful of criteria like

57
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:36,520
this. And it turns out that instead of asking the LLM to grade something on a scale of one to five,

58
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:42,960
which it tends not to be well calibrated on, if you instead give it say five binary criteria,

59
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:49,060
five zero one criteria, and have it give five binary scores, and you add up those scores to get the

60
00:05:49,060 --> 00:05:53,920
number from one to five, or one to ten, if you have ten binary criteria, that tends to give more

61
00:05:53,920 --> 00:06:02,120
consistent results. And so if were to gather a handful, say 10, 15 user queries for different

62
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:08,140
visualizations that the user may want to have of the coffee machine sales, then you can have it

63
00:06:08,140 --> 00:06:14,340
generate images without reflection, or generate images with reflection, and use a rubric like this

64
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:21,140
to score each of the images to then check the degree to which or whether or not the images generated

65
00:06:21,140 --> 00:06:26,460
with reflection are really better than the ones without reflection. And then once you've built up

66
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:32,480
a set of evals like this, if ever you want to change the initial generation prompt or you want to change

67
